{
  "input": "Was it ever rejected to let a TypedDict mark individual keys as required or optional, and if so, can I do it now and since which Python version?",
  "input_type": "question",
  "verdict": "explained",
  "summary": "TypedDict was introduced by PEP 589 (\"TypedDict: Type Hints for Dictionaries with a Fixed Set of Keys\", Python 3.8) to address: dict value types depend on the key (JSON-like objects). Alternatives considered and turned down along the way: \"There is no way to individually specify whether each key is required or not.\" (PEP 589: No proposed syntax was clear enough, and we expect that there is limited need for this.); \"TypedDict can't be used for specifying the type of a kwargs argument.\" (PEP 589: TypedDict can't be used for specifying the type of a kwargs argument.); \"TypedDict doesn't support providing a default value type for keys that are not explicitly…\" (PEP 589: TypedDict doesn't support providing a default value type for keys that are not explicitly defined.) PEP 589 left this out (\"There is no way to individually specify whether each key is required or not.\": No proposed syntax was clear enough, and we expect that there is limited need for this.); PEP 655 (\"Marking individual TypedDict items as required or potentially-missing\") later delivered it (a TypedDict cannot mark individual keys as required or optional) in Python 3.11. A closely related idea, \"Special syntax around the key of a TypedDict item\", was rejected in PEP 655: : This notation would require Python grammar changes and it is not believed that marking TypedDict items as required or potentially-missing would meet the high bar required to make such grammar changes. A closely related idea, \"Preventing unspecified keys in TypedDicts\", was rejected in PEP 705: Technically, however, this code is unsound: B does not declare foo, but instances of B may still have the key present, and the associated value may be of any type: mypy rejects the definition of get_field on the marked l",
  "minimum_python_version": "3.11",
